-is-math-real-how-simple-questions-lead-us-to-mathematics-deepest-truths-eugenia-cheng.jpg","readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0.092,"accessibilityScore":0.062,"scholarlyScore":0,"traits":[{"id":"narrative","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"quest\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Math is learned primarily through human curiosity—especially by asking questions—rather than by absorbing fixed textbook rules or purely following logic to reach a single right answer.","confidence":0.74},"academicOrientation":{"score":40,"confidence":0.45},"profileConfidence":0.8,"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"6f9dcf938d109241371acac5758bf464f069b2b3ca9ffbffb088ef3abd757f4a","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Science","secondarySubjects":[],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily explores mathematical concepts and human curiosity around them, fitting squarely within the Science subject, particularly focusing on Mathematics.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"9ab74ea82a0bbc4de52666d869bef31c6b4935ed2ebf79dfc8959de94dc068fe","summary":"One of the world's most creative mathematicians offers a new way to look at math--focusing on questions, not answers Where do we learn math: From rules in a textbook? From logic and deduction? Not really, according to mathematician Eugenia Cheng: we learn it from human curiosity--most importantly, from asking questions. This may come as a surprise to those who think that math is about finding the one right answer, or those who were told that the \"dumb\" question they asked just proved they were bad at math. But Cheng shows why people who ask questions like \"Why does 1 + 1 = 2?\" are at the very heart of the search for mathematical truth. Is Math Real? is a much-needed repudiation of the rigid ways we're taught to do math, and a celebration of the true, curious spirit of the discipline. Written with intelligence and passion, Is Math Real? brings us math as we've never seen it before, revealing how profound insights can emerge from seemingly unlikely sources.","publicationYear":2023,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"Mathematics","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-is-math-real-how-simple-questions-lead-us-to-mathematics-deepest-truths-eugenia-cheng"}],"displaySummary":"From logic and deduction? Not really, according to mathematician Eugenia Cheng: we learn it from human curiosity--most importantly, from asking questions. This may come as a surprise to those who think that math is about finding the one right answer, or those who were told that the \"dumb\" question they asked just proved they were bad at math. But Cheng shows why people who ask questions like \"Why does 1 + 1 = 2?\" are at the very heart of the search for mathematical truth. Is Math Real? is a much-needed repudiation of the rigid ways we're taught to do math, and a celebration of the true, curious spirit of the discipline. Written with intelligence and passion, Is Math Real? brings us math as we've never seen it before, revealing how profound insights can emerge from seemingly unlikely sources.","primaryTopic":"Science & Discovery","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Science","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Science","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Science","mappedSubject":"Science","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily explores mathematical concepts and human curiosity around them, fitting squarely within the Science subject, particularly focusing on Mathematics.","id":"subject-evidence-book-is-math-real-how-simple-questions-lead-us-to-mathematics-deepest-truths-eugenia-cheng-1"},{